{"is_success":true,"content":"搜索关键词：世界银行汶川地震重建项目评估\n\n网页标题：【外资司支持世界银行开展汶川地震灾后恢复重建项目评估】-国家发展和改革委员会\n\n网页链接：https://www.ndrc.gov.cn/fzggw/jgsj/wzs/sjjdt/201908/t20190826_1037000_ext.html\n\n发布时间：2019-08-26T17:21:17+08:00\n\n网页内容：外资司支持世界银行开展汶川地震灾后恢复重建项目评估\n8月23日下午，外资司应约接受世界银行独立评价局项目绩效评估报告任务组组长stephenhutton一行评估访谈，向世行介绍了汶川地震灾后恢复重建基本情况，对世行贷款汶川地震灾后恢复重建项目进行评价并提出建议。\n外资司介绍，在党中央国务院的领导下，我有力有序地组织开展了抗震救灾工作，通过各地政府对口支援、社会各界捐赠参与、震区人民积极建设，用两年时间提前完成汶川地震灾后恢复重建主要任务。在恢复重建过程中，国际社会和国际金融组织通过援助和优惠贷款等多种形式，发挥了积极重要作用。\n外资司认为，世行不仅为灾区恢复重建提供资金帮助，还在项目设计、建设规范、建设质量、运营维护和能力建设等多方面提供支持，通过提升防灾减灾能力实现社会效益、经济效益与环境效益的和谐统一。鉴于救灾本身的应急属性，外资司建议世行研究简化救灾项目的贷款审批流程，使世行在全球有关地区灾情发生后第一时间发挥其应有作用。\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：世界银行汶川地震重建项目评估\n\n网页标题：Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\n\n网页链接：http://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/104521468242672224/pdf/ISR-Disclosable-P114107-06-13-2016-1465827607155.pdf\n\n网页内容：Project Development Objective (from Project Appraisal Document) To restore essential infrastructure, health, and education services to at least levels existing prior to the Wenchuan Earthquake, and where appropriate, to provide for expansion of services, while reducing the vulnerability to seismic and flood hazards and building capacity of local governments to manage the recovery program.\nProgress towards achievement of PDO  Moderately Satisfactory  Moderately Satisfactory\nOverall Implementation Progress (IP)  Moderately Satisfactory  Moderately Satisfactory\nOverall Risk Rating  Moderate  Moderate\nImplementation Status and Key Decisions\nThis is the last ISR of the Project as the loan is closing on May 31, 2016. In Sichuan Province, restoration of essential infrastructure is largely satisfactory with 100% of water supply services completed and 95% of the roads have been completed, with only one road extending beyond loan closing. The health facilities have been 100% restored and are fully operational. The project financed health and water facilities are operating at levels higher than before the earthquake. The vulnerability to flood and seismic hazard of all project financed infrastructure has been reduced through much improved seismic engineering standards and relocating facilities to less vulnerable sites. The 2013 Lushan Earthquake affected project areas, but the infrastructure and facilities financed by the project withstood the earthquake and little damage was reported compared to other infrastructure in the region. The health and infrastructure agencies at the county level benefitted from training to reduce vulnerability to natural hazards and improve operation and maintenance practices. In Gansu Province, restoration of essential water infrastructure has reached 90% of the expected total. The Moba Gorge Dam will be completed after loan closing by August 2016, and only than the Cheng County water supply treatment plant can be connected to the reservoir and become operations. There have been some changes in the location and alignment of urban roads in Cheng County and Wudu City since not all resettlement issues could be resolved. Almost all project financed schools, hospitals, water and wastewater treatment plants in Gansu are now operational. However there is one school in Wan County that still needs to be connected to the water supply system financed by the County to become fully operational. The client should now prepare the final Borrower's completion report and assist the Bank with data collection and analysis for delivery of the final completion report in late November 2016. Much higher seismic standards were applied to project financed infrastructure and investments, less vulnerable sites were selected and in some cases specific infrastructure was financed to reduce exposure to flood risks. The capacity of health, education and infrastructure agencies was strengthened in the planning and implementation of infrastructure projects and reduce vulnerability to natural disasters.\nThe World Bank\nWenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\nThe World Bank\nWenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\nThe World Bank Implementation Status \u0026 Results Report Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\n Sichuan - Percentage of county towns with water supply services that are at a minimum restored to pre-earthquake levels (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 100.00 100.00 100.00\nDate 12-May-2008 28-Oct-2015 20-May-2016 30-Jun-2014\nPHINDPDOTBL\n Sichuan - Percentage of reconstructed (a) county health facilities and (b) township health facilities that reach bed occupancy rates not below 2007 levels (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 100.00 100.00 100.00\nDate 12-May-2008 28-Oct-2015 20-May-2016 30-Jun-2014\nPHINDPDOTBL\n Gansu - Percentage of project roads with traffic restored to pre-earthquake levels (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 90.00 95.00 100.00\nThe World Bank Implementation Status \u0026 Results Report Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\n6/13/2016 Page 7 of 12\nPublic Disclosure Copy\nPublic Disclosure Copy\nDate 12-May-2008 28-Oct-2015 20-May-2016 31-May-2016\nComments There were some changes in alignment and location of roads to reduce resettlement requirements.\nPHINDPDOTBL\n Gansu - Percentage of county towns with water supply services that are at a minimum restored to pre-earthquake levels (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 80.00 85.00 100.00\nDate 12-May-2008 28-Oct-2015 20-May-2016 31-May-2016\nComments Only the Cheng County Water Treatment Plant is not operational, waiting the completion of the Moba Gorge Reservoir.\nPHINDPDOTBL\n Gansu - Percentage of reconstructed (a) county health facilities and (b) township health facilities that reach bed occupancy rates not below 2007 levels (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 90.00 100.00 100.00\nDate 12-May-2008 28-Oct-2015 20-May-2016 31-May-2016\nComments All hospitals are fully operational, and the Cheng County Hospital was expanded is now also functioning normally.\nPHINDPDOTBL\n Gansu - Percentage of reconstruction (a) primary and (b) secondary schools that reach enrollment rates of (a) 99% and (b) 80% respectively (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 85.00 100.00 100.00\nDate 12-May-2008 28-Oct-2015 20-May-2016 31-May-2016\nComments The Number 3 School in Wan County is still lacking access to the locally financed water supply system, affecting its operation.\nThe World Bank Implementation Status \u0026 Results...\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：世界银行汶川地震重建项目评估\n\n网页标题：China: Building Back Stronger\n\n网页链接：https://www.worldbank.org/en/results/2018/05/11/building-back-better-plus\n\n发布时间：2018-05-11T00:00:00+08:00\n\n网页内容：Results Briefs May 11, 2018\nChina: Building Back Stronger\nThe Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery Project restored and improved critical infrastructure, health, and education services in the severely affected counties in China’s Sichuan and Gansu provinces, directly benefiting about 12.7 million people. The project helped build the capacity of local governments, and laid the foundation for more sustainable development and improving the quality of life and resilience of communities.\nPost-Wenchuan Earthquake Reconstruction and Development, Ten Years On\nChallenge\nOn May 12, 2008, a 8.01 magnitude earthquake struck southwestern China, centered in Wenchuan County of Sichuan Province. The effects of the earthquake were widespread across six provinces--Sichuan, Gansu, Shaanxi, Henan, Yunnan, and Hubei. With over 69,000 fatalities, 374,000 people injured, and about 18,000 missing, this was one of the deadliest earthquakes in recent history.\nAsset damage was severe and widespread in the six provinces, including: 34,000 km of highways destroyed; 1,263 reservoirs damaged; 7,444 schools and 11,028 hospitals and clinics in a state of collapse; and the houses of more than 4 million families were either destroyed or damaged. The earthquake and the large number of secondary disasters caused extensive ecological damage, including landslides, the formation of barrier lakes and destruction of forests. Direct economic costs were assessed at a staggering RMB 844 billion (US$123 billion).\nThe two most affected provinces were Sichuan and Gansu, with infrastructure, health and education sectors suffering the most damage. The severity of the Wenchuan Earthquake posed a significant threat to development in both provinces, and without intervention, unemployment would likely increase and large numbers of people could slip back below the poverty line.\nApproach\nFollowing the earthquake, the World Bank acted quickly and offered its global experience and knowledge in addressing post-disaster challenges to the Chinese government. The Bank mobilized grants to provide technical assistance for the national reconstruction planning and emergency tents for the relief effort. Exactly one month after the earthquake, the Bank organized a workshop that brought the world's top experts on post-earthquake reconstruction to Beijing. The Bank also provided three policy notes and 17 sector-specific Good Practice Notes.\nAt the request of the government, the Bank quickly prepared an emergency loan to support the restoration and enhancement of basic infrastructure, health and education services in the provinces of Sichuan and Gansu. The project aimed to reduce the vulnerability to seismic and flood hazards and build the capacity of local governments to manage the recovery program and infrastructure.\nThe project took a “Build Back Better Plus” approach that ensured that all project construction activities used higher seismic-proof standards and flood risk management codes, while ensuring a balance between reconstruction activities and laying a foundation for the longer-term sustainable economic recovery and development of the project towns. For the first time in China, a flexible framework approach was used which allowed the provinces to identify and prepare subprojects efficiently.\n12.7 million\nThe Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery Project restored and improved critical infrastructure, health, and education services in severely affected counties in China’s Sichuan and Gansu provinces, directly benefiting about 12.7 million people.\nResults\nThe project was implemented in 27 severely affected counties in Sichuan and Gansu between 2009 and 2014, and restored and enhanced access to essential infrastructure and other public services, such as water, sanitation, roads, health and education, but also greatly enhanced the services from pre-earthquake levels and contributed to rapid recovery and growth of the local economies, specifically:\nRoads. 300 roads were built or renovated to new seismic standards, with new traffic management system, drainage, lighting and landscaping.\n![](https://www.worldbank.org/content/dam/photos/780x439/2018/may-1/cn-wenchuan4.jpg)\nWater. Ten water supply plants were built with 230 km of water supply pipelines and four pumping stations, improving access to households and commercial and industrial users, as well as for urban fire-fighting purposes. This led to an increase of 25% in coverage of households and commercial buildings with a continuous 24-hour water supply compared to the pre-earthquake situation.\nWastewater. Nine wastewater treatment plants were rehabilitated or constructed, and 102.5 km of sewerage pipelines and 492.5 km of storm water drainage pipelines were built. As a result, the sewage treatment ratio in the project counties increased by over 50% compared to the pre-earthquake situation.\nSolid waste collection and disposal (Sichuan only). Investments under this project led to an average increase of 42% in the collection and disposal ratio of solid waste garbage compared to the pre-earthquake situation.\nHealth. 67 health facilities were built, equipped and are fully operational. The number of hospital beds in the project counties increased by 67% and 35% in Sichuan and Gansu respectively. The new facilities has greatly enhanced diagnostic and treatment capacities and provide a wider range of in-patient and out-patient medical services, resulting in higher admissions than before the earthquake.\n![](https://www.worldbank.org/content/dam/photos/780x439/2018/may-1/cn-wenchuan3.jpg)\nEducation (Gansu Only). Seven new schools were built and equipped with text books, computer equipment and laboratories, classroom, canteen and dormitory furniture, and sporting equipment. Training courses were provided for 450 principals, teachers and administrators, and covered a broad range of education topics, including new emergency management procedures.\nCapacity building. The provincial, municipal and county level governments of both provinces benefited from a broad range of training and capacity building activities related to the World Bank’s safeguards, financial management and procurement, as well as technical areas such as investigation, design and contract supervision, with a total of 3,701 training days completed.\nEconomic growth. Compared with pre-earthquake level, GDP grew between 117% and 198%, primarily driven by growth in the non-agricultural sectors. Secondary industries (including both industrial and construction sectors) increased between 116% and 286% and service sectors grew by 135% and 201%. Per capita income across the board increased between 90% and 154% in project counties.\nBank Group Contribution\nA US$710 million Emergency Loan was provided by the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD) to support recovery investments in Sichuan (US$510 million) and Gansu (US$200 million), focusing on the reconstruction of infrastructure such as roads, bridges, water supply, wastewater, solid waste, and health facilities in selected counties in both provinces, and also on the reconstruction of education facilities in Gansu.\nThe Bank mobilized US$1.5 million grant from the Global Facility for Disaster Reduction and Recovery (GFDRR) to support the preparation of policy notes, the involvement of international experts, and the training of teachers and school staff, as well as...\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：世界银行汶川地震重建项目评估\n\n网页标题：项目信息文件 评估阶段\n\n网页链接：http://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/646691468220510991/pdf/AB42910PID0CHI1overy1Project1PID1cn.pdf\n\n网页内容：publicdisclosureauthorizedpublicdisclosureauthorizedpublicdisclosureauthorizedpublicdisclosureauthorized项目信息文件评估阶段报告编号:ab4291项目名称汶川地震恢复项目所属地区东亚与太平洋地区所属行业一般性供水､环境卫生和防洪(44%);道路与公路(39%);卫生(13%);一般性教育(4%)项目号p114107借款人中华人民共和国执行机构四川省政府四川省发展和改革委员会联系人:杨欣电话:+8613980552272联系人:雷欣电话:+869318891038甘肃省政府甘肃省财政厅环境类别a项目信息文件编制2008年10月31日日期授权评估日期2008年11月12日执董会审批日期2008年12月16日1､国别与行业背景2008年5月12日,中国西南部发生8.0级地震,震中位于四川省汶川县｡汶川地震和其后3万多次较强余震波及4700多万人,6.9万多人死亡,37.4万人受伤,1.8万人失踪｡地震破坏了3.4万公里的公路和1263座水库,震塌了7444所学校､11028家医院和诊所,550万农村住房和86万城市住房倒塌或严重受损｡直接经济损失估计达8440亿元(合1230亿美元)｡12008年5月后,中国政府行使强有力的领导职责,组织初期的救灾工作,开展受损和需求评估并实施初期重建规划｡初期重建规划包括九个部分,内容涉及城市建设规划､住房､农村发展和农业､基础设施､公共服务(如社会保障､卫生和教育)､土地利用规划､市场和金融服务､企事业单位､灾害风险降低和生态恢复以及一个全方位的《国家汶川地震灾后恢复重建总体规划》｡国务院在广泛征求公众意见后,于2008年8月27日批准《总体规划》｡据《总体规划》测算,重建成本将达1万亿人民币左右(合1470亿美元),主要重建工程约在三年内完成｡1本段中的数据引自中国政府的《汶川地震灾后恢复重建总体规划》｡1四川甘肃两省受灾程度最严重,重建需求高,占整个工程的比例很大｡四川省的39个县在汶川地震中的损失最大,这些县大多位于四川省西南部,损失额占全部直接经济损失2的91.3%,估计达1122亿美元｡四川省面积为48.5万平方公里,有181个县,占中国西部人口和全国gdp的三分之一,因其工农业生产力水平高,足以养活当地密集的人口,被誉为中国的“粮仓”｡四川省有着优越的战略地理位置､辽阔的面积､丰富的自然资源和巨大的经济发展潜力,是中国政府西部大开发工程不可或缺的组成部分｡甘肃省总人口为2600万,按人均gdp衡量,是全国第二大贫困省｡这主要是因为其地形不利和基础设施不足｡甘肃省面积达45.4万平方公里,有86个县,但受灾地区主要是与四川相邻的东南8县｡甘肃省损失额占全部直接经济损失的5.8%,估计达71亿美元｡3四川甘肃两省受灾县的灾后重建至关重要,涉及到确保当地的长期发展目标和避免人民再度陷入贫困｡商务部预计震后受灾地区仍有1千多万人可能再次生活在贫困线以下｡4在《总体规划》的指导下,中国政府已批准向国际金融机构借款15亿美元的计划,其中约12亿美元将用于四川省的重建,2亿美元用于甘肃,1亿美元用于陕西｡为获取国际复兴开发银行贷款而拟议的本紧急项目将针对基础设施､卫生和教育等方面具体的重建需求｡这些需求由四川和甘肃两省的省政府和县政府按照《总体规划》的指导方针和框架提出｡2､项目目标项目目标是要将基本的基础设施､卫生和教育服务至少恢复到汶川地震前的水平,如果有条件,要提供更多的服务,增强抗震和防洪能力｡3､世行介入本项目的理由世行在全球灾后重建､管理和预防等方面有着数十年的经验,有能力在重建过程中向中国政府提供大规模援助,尤其是在省级层面上帮助地方政府监督中国前所未有的大型重建工作｡此外,国际复兴开发银行对华贷款额度一直较大而且稳定,所以在中国政府《总体规划》中需要向国际借款的所有行业,世行均有工作经验｡虽然中国政府也欢迎其它金融机构参与重建工作,但估计重建项目中向世行借款的行业数目最多､地理范围最广｡拟议项目作为世行在中国的第一笔大型规划贷款项目,将引入创新型项目特点｡2引自汶川地震专家委员会史培军先生的报告,新华网,2008年9月4日｡3引自汶川地震专家委员会史培军先生的报告,新华网,2008年9月4日｡4http://www.npfpc.gov.cn/en/en2008-07/enews20080716.htm｡2世行参与重建的另一优势在于世行有着在全球开展灾后重建项目的经验教训并积累了许多有关最佳实践的知识,从而能将重建工作打包成一个战略项目,包括一整套技术援助赠款和投资贷款｡2008年5月后国际社会向中国政府提供的支持中也有世行的一份功劳,世行通过全球减灾与重建基金､全球环境基金和意大利文化可持续发展信托基金筹集了292.5万美元的信托基金｡世行也为中国政府提供了大量的技术援助,如2008年6月12日与财政部共同组织召开了“汶川地震灾后重建国际经验和政策建议介绍会”｡会后世行又即刻为重建规划提供了17个行业最佳实践的经验,为《总体规划》的编制提供了借鉴｡世行最近批准了op/bp8.00条款《对危机和紧急状态的迅速回应》,简化了程序,这将有助于加快项目准备审批进程｡4､项目描述拟议项目采用了框架法,即在评估工作开始前用以确定项目原则和子项目选择标准的较为灵活的项目设计方法,但并非所有投资项目都能在执董会批准钱得以明确并评估｡拟议项目可能包括以下三大类子项目:(1)项目下可适用追溯贷款的子项目;(2)直接重建项目;(3)需要更长时间和更细致准备工作的复杂投资项目...\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：世界银行汶川地震重建项目评估\n\n网页标题：Supporting Post-earthquake Recovery in China\n\n网页链接：https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2012/12/04/supporting-post-earthquake-recovery-in-china\n\n发布时间：2012-12-04T00:00:00+08:00\n\n网页内容：The Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery Project (2009-2014) is restoring and building high quality infrastructure and improving health and education services for residents in the areas affected by the devastating earthquake in 2008, laying a solid foundation for long-term economic and social development of participating cities and counties in Sichuan and Gansu provinces.\nChallenges\nOn May 12, 2008, an earthquake of magnitude 8.0 struck southwestern China, centered in Wenchuan County of Sichuan Province. As a result, more than 47 million people were affected; over 69,000 people died, 374,000 were injured, and 18,000 went missing.\nPublic infrastructure, key government buildings and houses were severely damaged: 34,000 km of highway destroyed, 1,263 reservoirs damaged, 7,444 schools and 11,028 hospitals and clinics collapsed, and 5.5 million rural houses and 860,000 urban houses destroyed or significantly damaged. Sichuan and Gansu provinces were the hardest hit, with direct economic losses estimated at US$112.2 billion and US$7.1 billion respectively.\nApproach\nIn order to plan for reconstruction in an efficient and effective manner, loss assessments, geological assessments and environmental assessments were conducted immediately after the earthquake. China’s State Council issued the Overall Plan for the Post-Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery and Reconstruction which lays out the framework for all recovery and reconstruction efforts.\nInnovative mechanisms to secure the funds necessary to conduct these works, such as private-public partnerships, tax incentives, and the mobilization of public support are utilized thoroughly. The central government also set up a “twin assistance” mechanism by which affected counties are paired with a donor province responsible for offering financial and other assistance. The program also emphasized the non-structural aspects of building resilience in communities through better emergency preparedness and training programs.\nWorld Bank support\nTo assist the earthquake-affected people to carry out the post-disaster reconstruction and to implement the Wenchuan Earthquake Reconstruction Master Plan, the Chinese government borrowed an emergency loan of US$710 million for the Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery Project (WERP). WERP is being implemented in both Gansu Province (US$200 million) and Sichuan Province (US$510 million).\nWERP supports reconstruction and expansion of infrastructure including roads and bridges, water supply and sanitation, municipal solid waste management collection and disposal, drainage, flood control, slope protection and stabilization, and restoration of waterways and canals, as well as health facilities including township health centers, maternal and child health centers, and county hospitals and community disease control centers in both Sichuan and Gansu. In addition, the project finances facilities, equipment and selective capacity building for the rehabilitation and reconstruction of primary schools, secondary schools, and vocational/technical schools in Gansu Province. The objective is to restore infrastructure, health, and education services to at least levels existing prior to the earthquake, and where appropriate, to provide for expansion of services while reducing the vulnerability to seismic and flood hazards.\nIn addition, the International Finance Corporation (IFC), the private sector arm of the World Bank Group, provided advice to local government and financial institutions, including Mianyang City Commercial Bank (MCCB) to strengthen the post-disaster investment climate, attract outside investors, and support the reconstruction. IFC invested in six projects with a total commitment of $120 million, including $1.6 million equity in MCCB’s subsidiary, Beichuan Fumin Village and Township Bank, to expand access to finance for farmers and micro and small businesses.\nThe Global Facility for Disaster Reduction and Recovery (GFDRR) supported the involvement of international experts in the preparation of the WERP, and the training of teachers and school staff, as well as hospital staff, to improve emergency preparedness and response in schools and hospitals. (link to GFDDR Website – China: Restoring and Improving Education in Earthquake-Struck Areas – https://www.gfdrr.org/gfdrr/node/1180). The GFDRR also supported the government to review and evaluate the implementation of the Wenchuan Earthquake Reconstruction Master Plan and six sector-specific: rural reconstruction, urban and rural housing, public services and infrastructure, industrial recovery, relocation, and organization and implementation. The summary report was published in June 2012 and is available on the GFDRR website: https://www.gfdrr.org/gfdrr/node/1289.\nThe Global Environment Facility (GEF) provided a grant of US$1 million to support assessment of potential chemical contamination from industries affected by the earthquake.\n\" Now with this road rebuilt, my business is a lot better. There is less dust and noise. I hope more good roads will be built, to make our city a better place to live. \"\na taxi driver...\nWERP has made substantial progress since its launch around mid-2009. The Bank financing have leverage additional investments and helped transform cities, improving services and livelihood of people and improving the quality of the overall environment. Specific results include:\nConstruction of 110 km of roads, 100 km storm water pipelines, 93 km of sewers, 2 km of bridges, 9 km of embankments, and 105 km of water supply pipelines, 2 pumping stations, 1 solid waste transfer station, 3 solid waste storage stations, and 2 water supply plants in Sichuan Province;\nReconstruction and expansion of township health centers, maternal and child health centers, county hospitals and community disease control centers in 19 counties in Sichuan. By November 2012, all 60 health facilities were completed and are now fully operational, benefiting 9.82 million people.\nConstruction of 31 civil works (infrastructure investments in buildings, roads, water and sanitation), six schools, four hospitals, one bridge, one water treatment plant, and two wastewater treatment plants in Gansu Province;\nFacilities are now upgraded to conform to environmental standards and ultimately minimize pollution. With support from the World Bank, the government is repairing the natural environment damaged by the earthquake thereby preserving the ecological environment of the area as well as contributing to the mitigation of future disasters.\nSpecific measures are taken to ensure that the cultural heritage and the distinctive local flavor of the cities of the af­fected areas are preserved.\nToward the Future\nThe World Bank will continue to strengthen its partnership with the Government of China on disaster risk management activities and disseminate China’s experience and expertise in disaster preparedness and relief to other countries in the region and worldwide. With support from GFDRR through the World Bank, the China Emergency Relief Training Center (CERT) offered a one-week “Emergency Response and Relief Training” in Beijing to selected rescue teams from Indonesia. Likewise a full report on the learning from the reconstruction has been published for international dissemination.\nBeneficiaries\n\n\n========\n搜索关键词：World Bank Sichuan earthquake reconstruction evaluation\n\n网页标题：Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\n\n网页链接：http://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/104521468242672224/pdf/ISR-Disclosable-P114107-06-13-2016-1465827607155.pdf\n\n网页内容：Project Development Objective (from Project Appraisal Document) To restore essential infrastructure, health, and education services to at least levels existing prior to the Wenchuan Earthquake, and where appropriate, to provide for expansion of services, while reducing the vulnerability to seismic and flood hazards and building capacity of local governments to manage the recovery program.\nHas the Project Development Objective been changed since Board Approval of the Project Objective? No\nPHRPDODEL\nName Previous Rating Current Rating\nComponents\nName\n1.1 Infrastructure Sichuan:(Cost $432.40 M)\n1.2 Health Sichuan:(Cost $57.60 M)\n1.3 Project Support Sichuan:(Cost $20.00 M)\n2.1 Infrastructure Gansu:(Cost $130.30 M)\n2.2 Health Gansu:(Cost $32.30 M)\n2.3 Education Gansu:(Cost $33.40 M)\n2.4 Project Support Gansu:(Cost $4.00 M)\nOverall Ratings\nPublic Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized\nThe World Bank Implementation Status \u0026 Results Report Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\n6/13/2016 Page 2 of 12\nPublic Disclosure Copy\nPublic Disclosure Copy\nProgress towards achievement of PDO  Moderately Satisfactory  Moderately Satisfactory\nOverall Implementation Progress (IP)  Moderately Satisfactory  Moderately Satisfactory\nOverall Risk Rating  Moderate  Moderate\nImplementation Status and Key Decisions\nThis is the last ISR of the Project as the loan is closing on May 31, 2016. In Sichuan Province, restoration of essential infrastructure is largely satisfactory with 100% of water supply services completed and 95% of the roads have been completed, with only one road extending beyond loan closing. The health facilities have been 100% restored and are fully operational. The project financed health and water facilities are operating at levels higher than before the earthquake. The vulnerability to flood and seismic hazard of all project financed infrastructure has been reduced through much improved seismic engineering standards and relocating facilities to less vulnerable sites. The 2013 Lushan Earthquake affected project areas, but the infrastructure and facilities financed by the project withstood the earthquake and little damage was reported compared to other infrastructure in the region. The health and infrastructure agencies at the county level benefitted from training to reduce vulnerability to natural hazards and improve operation and maintenance practices. In Gansu Province, restoration of essential water infrastructure has reached 90% of the expected total. The Moba Gorge Dam will be completed after loan closing by August 2016, and only than the Cheng County water supply treatment plant can be connected to the reservoir and become operations. There have been some changes in the location and alignment of urban roads in Cheng County and Wudu City since not all resettlement issues could be resolved. Almost all project financed schools, hospitals, water and wastewater treatment plants in Gansu are now operational. However there is one school in Wan County that still needs to be connected to the water supply system financed by the County to become fully operational. The client should now prepare the final Borrower's completion report and assist the Bank with data collection and analysis for delivery of the final completion report in late November 2016. Much higher seismic standards were applied to project financed infrastructure and investments, less vulnerable sites were selected and in some cases specific infrastructure was financed to reduce exposure to flood risks. The capacity of health, education and infrastructure agencies was strengthened in the planning and implementation of infrastructure projects and reduce vulnerability to natural disasters.\nThe World Bank\nWenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\nThe World Bank\nWenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\nThe World Bank Implementation Status \u0026 Results Report Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\n Gansu - Percentage of project roads with traffic restored to pre-earthquake levels (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 90.00 95.00 100.00\nThe World Bank Implementation Status \u0026 Results Report Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery (P114107)\n6/13/2016 Page 7 of 12\nPublic Disclosure Copy\nPublic Disclosure Copy\nDate 12-May-2008 28-Oct-2015 20-May-2016 31-May-2016\nComments There were some changes in alignment and location of roads to reduce resettlement requirements.\nPHINDPDOTBL\n Gansu - Percentage of county towns with water supply services that are at a minimum restored to pre-earthquake levels (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 80.00 85.00 100.00\nDate 12-May-2008 28-Oct-2015 20-May-2016 31-May-2016\nComments Only the Cheng County Water Treatment Plant is not operational, waiting the completion of the Moba Gorge Reservoir.\nPHINDPDOTBL\n Gansu - Percentage of reconstructed (a) county health facilities and (b) township health facilities that reach bed occupancy rates not below 2007 levels (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 90.00 100.00 100.00\nDate 12-May-2008 28-Oct-2015 20-May-2016 31-May-2016\nComments All hospitals are fully operational, and the Cheng County Hospital was expanded is now also functioning normally.\nPHINDPDOTBL\n Gansu - Percentage of reconstruction (a) primary and (b) secondary schools that reach enrollment rates of (a) 99% and (b) 80% respectively (Percentage, Custom)\nBaseline Actual (Previous) Actual (Current) End Target\nValue 0.00 85.00 100.00 100.00\nDate 12-May-2008 28-Oct-...\nIn addition, water treatment plants, wastewater treatment plants and/or pumping stations were built in 6 Counties in Sichuan and 3 Counties in Gansu.\nOverall Comments\nData on Financial Performance\nDisbursements (by loan)\nProject Loan/Credit/TF Status Currency Original Revised Cancelled Disbursed Undisbursed Disbursed\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：World Bank Sichuan earthquake reconstruction evaluation\n\n网页标题：China: Building Back Stronger\n\n网页链接：https://www.worldbank.org/en/results/2018/05/11/building-back-better-plus\n\n发布时间：2018-05-11T00:00:00+08:00\n\n网页内容：Following the earthquake, the World Bank acted quickly and offered its global experience and knowledge in addressing post-disaster challenges to the Chinese government. The Bank mobilized grants to provide technical assistance for the national reconstruction planning and emergency tents for the relief effort. Exactly one month after the earthquake, the Bank organized a workshop that brought the world's top experts on post-earthquake reconstruction to Beijing. The Bank also provided three policy notes and 17 sector-specific Good Practice Notes.\nAt the request of the government, the Bank quickly prepared an emergency loan to support the restoration and enhancement of basic infrastructure, health and education services in the provinces of Sichuan and Gansu. The project aimed to reduce the vulnerability to seismic and flood hazards and build the capacity of local governments to manage the recovery program and infrastructure.\nThe project took a “Build Back Better Plus” approach that ensured that all project construction activities used higher seismic-proof standards and flood risk management codes, while ensuring a balance between reconstruction activities and laying a foundation for the longer-term sustainable economic recovery and development of the project towns. For the first time in China, a flexible framework approach was used which allowed the provinces to identify and prepare subprojects efficiently.\n12.7 million\nThe Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery Project restored and improved critical infrastructure, health, and education services in severely affected counties in China’s Sichuan and Gansu provinces, directly benefiting about 12.7 million people.\nResults\nThe project was implemented in 27 severely affected counties in Sichuan and Gansu between 2009 and 2014, and restored and enhanced access to essential infrastructure and other public services, such as water, sanitation, roads, health and education, but also greatly enhanced the services from pre-earthquake levels and contributed to rapid recovery and growth of the local economies, specifically:\nRoads. 300 roads were built or renovated to new seismic standards, with new traffic management system, drainage, lighting and landscaping.\n![](https://www.worldbank.org/content/dam/photos/780x439/2018/may-1/cn-wenchuan4.jpg)\nWater. Ten water supply plants were built with 230 km of water supply pipelines and four pumping stations, improving access to households and commercial and industrial users, as well as for urban fire-fighting purposes. This led to an increase of 25% in coverage of households and commercial buildings with a continuous 24-hour water supply compared to the pre-earthquake situation.\nWastewater. Nine wastewater treatment plants were rehabilitated or constructed, and 102.5 km of sewerage pipelines and 492.5 km of storm water drainage pipelines were built. As a result, the sewage treatment ratio in the project counties increased by over 50% compared to the pre-earthquake situation.\nSolid waste collection and disposal (Sichuan only). Investments under this project led to an average increase of 42% in the collection and disposal ratio of solid waste garbage compared to the pre-earthquake situation.\nHealth. 67 health facilities were built, equipped and are fully operational. The number of hospital beds in the project counties increased by 67% and 35% in Sichuan and Gansu respectively. The new facilities has greatly enhanced diagnostic and treatment capacities and provide a wider range of in-patient and out-patient medical services, resulting in higher admissions than before the earthquake.\n![](https://www.worldbank.org/content/dam/photos/780x439/2018/may-1/cn-wenchuan3.jpg)\nEducation (Gansu Only). Seven new schools were built and equipped with text books, computer equipment and laboratories, classroom, canteen and dormitory furniture, and sporting equipment. Training courses were provided for 450 principals, teachers and administrators, and covered a broad range of education topics, including new emergency management procedures.\nCapacity building. The provincial, municipal and county level governments of both provinces benefited from a broad range of training and capacity building activities related to the World Bank’s safeguards, financial management and procurement, as well as technical areas such as investigation, design and contract supervision, with a total of 3,701 training days completed.\nEconomic growth. Compared with pre-earthquake level, GDP grew between 117% and 198%, primarily driven by growth in the non-agricultural sectors. Secondary industries (including both industrial and construction sectors) increased between 116% and 286% and service sectors grew by 135% and 201%. Per capita income across the board increased between 90% and 154% in project counties.\nBank Group Contribution\nA US$710 million Emergency Loan was provided by the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD) to support recovery investments in Sichuan (US$510 million) and Gansu (US$200 million), focusing on the reconstruction of infrastructure such as roads, bridges, water supply, wastewater, solid waste, and health facilities in selected counties in both provinces, and also on the reconstruction of education facilities in Gansu.\nThe Bank mobilized US$1.5 million grant from the Global Facility for Disaster Reduction and Recovery (GFDRR) to support the preparation of policy notes, the involvement of international experts, and the training of teachers and school staff, as well as hospital staff. The GFDRR grant also supported the government to review and evaluate the implementation of the national reconstruction materplan and six sector-specific reconstruction plans.\nThe Bank also mobilized a Global Environment Facility (GEF) grant of US$1 million to support assessment of potential chemical contamination from industries affected by the earthquake.\nThe International Finance Corporation (IFC), the private sector arm of the World Bank Group, created credit lines for small and medium-sized businesses in the aftermath of the earthquake.\nPartners\nIn addition to GFDRR and GEF, the French Development Agency (AfD) provided a grant to support early engagement of French consultants who worked with the World Bank team to develop selection criteria and appraise subprojects, and help build early capacity of provincial project management offices. Finally, a GEF grant supported the assessment of the chemical contamination in the immediate aftermath of the earthquake.\nMoving Forward\nThe project supported the training of post-disaster recovery teams in the Asia-Pacific region, strengthened the part­nership with the government of China on disaster risk management activities, and disseminate China’s experience and expertise in disaster preparedness and relief to other countries in the region and worldwide. A report on the lessons learned from the reconstruction has been published for international dissemination and many of the lessons from the Wenchuan Earthquake reconstruction are being applied elsewhere.\nThe Lushan Earthquake struck Sichuan in 2013, almost five years after the Wenchuan Earthquake, and affected 2.2 million people. The World Bank-supported Lushan Earthquake Reconstruction and Risk Reduction Project has learned and incorporated lessons from the Wenchuan Earthquake Recovery Project.\nTo this day, the Wenchuan Earthquake serves an important reminder to other countries prone to earthquakes of the importance of measures ensuring construction quality and structural resilience of buildings and infrastructure. Moreover, the recovery process, its efficacy, and speed in Sichuan, serves an example of the large-scale reconstruction that aims not only to replace the lost and damaged assets but also to bring the reconstructed and new infrastructure to a higher quality...\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：World Bank Sichuan earthquake reconstruction evaluation\n\n网页标题：助力中国灾后恢复重建\n\n网页链接：http://www.shihang.org/zh/news/feature/2012/12/04/supporting-post-earthquake-recovery-in-china\n\n发布时间：2012-12-04T00:00:00+08:00\n\n网页内容：助力中国灾后恢复重建\n2012年12月4日\n概述\n2008年汶川地震发生后，世行贷款汶川地震恢复重建项目（2009～2014）帮助四川、甘肃两省在灾区高标准、高质量地恢复重建基础设施，改善灾区居民的医疗卫生和教育服务，为项目市县未来的长期经济和社会发展打下牢固基础。\n挑战\n2008年5月12日发生的以四川省汶川县为震中的里氏8级特大地震造成470多万人受灾，死亡6.9万人，受伤37.4万人，失踪1.8万人。\n公共基础设施、主要政府大楼和房屋建筑遭到严重破坏：34,000公里的公路被毁；1263座水库受损；7444所学校和11028所医院和诊所倒塌；550万农村房屋和86万城市房屋被毁或受损严重。四川、甘肃两省受灾最严重，直接经济损失估计约相当于四川省1122亿美元，甘肃省71亿美元。\n方式\n为了及时高效地制定震后重建规划，中国政府在地震发生后迅速开展了灾害损失、地质和环境评估工作。国务院颁布的《汶川地震灾后恢复重建总体规划》为全力推进灾后恢复重建奠定了基础。\n中国政府采取了各种创新机制保障恢复重建所需要的资金，充分利用包括公共与私营部门合作、税收优惠、动员公众支持等各种方式。中央政府建立了灾后重建对口支援机制，承担对口支援任务的省市与各自对口支援的受灾县市“结对”，负责为其提供资金和其他方式的援助。除硬件建设外，灾后重建规划也很重视能力建设，通过组织加强应急准备和培训项目，建立加强社区的自然灾害抵御能力。\n世行支持\n为了帮助地震灾区人民开展灾后重建和落实《汶川地震灾后恢复重建总体规划》，世界银行向中国政府提供紧急贷款7.1亿美元，用于甘肃（2亿美元）和四川（5.1亿美元）两省的灾后恢复重建。\n汶川地震恢复重建项目为四川、甘肃两省灾区的受损基础设施的重建和扩建提供资金，建设内容包括道路桥梁、供水及环境卫生、城市固体废弃物管理和收集处理、排水系统、防洪设施、边坡保护及稳定、运河航道恢复等，还包括乡镇卫生院、妇幼保健中心、县医院、社区防疫中心等卫生设施。此外，项目还为甘肃省灾区中小学和职业技术学校的恢复重建提供设施设备和能力建设。项目的目标是基础设施、医疗卫生和教育服务至少恢复到地震前的水平，并在适当情况下扩大服务范围，提高服务质量，同时降低面临地震、洪水等自然灾害的脆弱性。\n此外，世界银行的私营部门窗口国际金融公司为四川地震灾区的地方政府和金融机构提供咨询建议，帮助在灾后加强投资环境、吸引外来投资和支持恢复重建。国际金融公司共投资了六个项目，总金额达1.2亿美元，包括出资160万美元参股绵阳市商业银行设立的北川富民村镇银行，扩大农户和微小企业的融资渠道。\n全球减灾与恢复基金（GFDRR）赠款资助国际专家参与汶川地震项目准备，并为项目区的学校教职员工和医疗机构工作人员组织培训，加强学校和医院的灾害应急准备和应对能力全球减灾与恢复基金还协助中国政府对《汶川地震灾后恢复重建总体规划》以及农村重建、城乡住房、公共服务与基础设施、工业恢复、搬迁安置、组织与实施等六个具体方面进行审议评价，2012年6月发布了概要报告。\n全球环境基金（GEF）提供赠款100万美元调查和评估地震引起的危险化学品和废料泄漏可能造成的环境污染。\n\" 现在这个路修好了，我的生意好了很多。现在灰尘少了，噪音小了。希望以后再多修一些这些路，把我们三台县整得更加美好。 \"\n三台县的一位出租车司机\n成果\n汶川地震恢复重建项目自2009年中启动以来取得了显著进展。世行资金也带动了其他渠道的投资，帮助改变了灾区城市面貌、改善了服务和人民生活，提高了整体环境质量。具体项目成果如下：\n在四川省恢复重建道路110公里、雨水排水管网100公里、污水管网93公里、桥梁2公里、堤坝105公里、泵站2座、垃圾中转站1座、垃圾存放站3座和自来水厂2座。\n在四川省19个县市重建和扩建乡镇卫生院、妇幼保健院、县医院和社区防疫中心。截止到2012年11月，60个医疗卫生项目全部完成和投入运营，受益人口达982万人。\n在甘肃省恢复重建房屋、道路、供排水管网和环境卫生基础设施项目31个、学校6所、医院4所、桥梁1座、自来水厂1座和污水处理厂2座。\n恢复重建后的各项设施符合环境标准，力求污染最小化。在世行协助下，政府修复了遭受地震破坏的自然环境，在保护当地生态系统的同时也有助于减少未来的灾害。\n对地震灾区的历史文化遗产和城市独特风貌采取了具体的保护措施。\n面向未来\n世界银行将继续加强与中国政府在灾害风险管理方面的合作伙伴关系，向其他国家传播中国在灾害准备及救援方面的经验。在通过世行提供的全球减灾与恢复基金支持下，中国紧急救援培训中心在北京为印尼一些救援队举办了为期一周的应急救援培训，还出版了重建经验报告在国际上推广。\n受益人感言\n三台县的一位出租车司机：“现在这个路修好了，我的生意好了很多。现在灰尘少了，噪音小了。希望以后再多修一些这些路，把我们三台县整得更加美好。”\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：World Bank Sichuan earthquake reconstruction evaluation\n\n网页标题：Lushan Earthquake Reconstruction Project (P153548) Implementation Completion Report (ICR) Review\n\n网页链接：https://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/099111124132042570/pdf/P15354815f1b7e04218b2114870e21711cd.pdf\n\n网页内容：Independent Evaluation Group (IEG) Implementation Completion Report (ICR) Review\nLushan Earthquake Reconstruction Project (P153548)\nPage 1 of 22\nReport Number: ICRR0024203\n1. Project Data\nProject ID Project Name\nP153548 Lushan Earthquake Reconstruction Project\nCountry Practice Area(Lead)\nChina Urban, Resilience and Land\nL/C/TF Number(s) Closing Date (Original) Total Project Cost (USD)\nIBRD-86470 30-Jun-2022 296,699,155.72\nBank Approval Date Closing Date (Actual)\n13-Oct-2016 31-Dec-2023\nIBRD/IDA (USD) Grants (USD)\nOriginal Commitment 300,000,000.00 0.00\nRevised Commitment 300,000,000.00 0.00\nActual 300,000,000.00 0.00\nPrepared by Reviewed by ICR Review Coordinator Group\nCynthia Nunez-Ollero Ebru Karamete Avjeet Singh IEGSD (Unit 4)\n2. Project Objectives and Components\nDEVOBJ_TBL\na. Objectives\nAccording to the Loan Agreement (LA, p. 5) and the Project Appraisal Document (PAD, paragraph 11), the Project Development Objectives (PDOs) of this Lushan Earthquake Reconstruction Project are “to improve access to disaster resilient infrastructure and strengthen risk reduction in select municipalities of Sichuan Province and to improve emergency preparedness in Shimian County in Sichuan Province.”\nThis review will assess project performance against the above objectives parsed into the following:\nPublic Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized\nIndependent Evaluation Group (IEG) Implementation Completion Report (ICR) Review\nLushan Earthquake Reconstruction Project (P153548)\nPage 2 of 22\nto improve access to disaster resilient infrastructure and strengthen risk reduction in select municipalities of Sichuan Province\nto improve emergency preparedness in Shimian County in Sichuan Province.\nA Level 1 restructuring (see below) revised the PDO to replace Shimian County (a third level local government administrative hierarchy) with Ya'an Municipality (a second level local government administrative hierarchy). Shimian county is located within Ya'an Municipality.\nb. Were the project objectives/key associated outcome targets revised during implementation?\nYes\nDid the Board approve the revised objectives/key associated outcome targets? No\nc. Will a split evaluation be undertaken?\nNo\nd. Components\n1. Upgrading and Risk Reduction of Rural Roads (Total cost at appraisal was US$47.61 million, of which US$29.95 million was from IBRD; restructuring reduced the cost to US$39.83 million, of which US$24.3 million was from IBRD; actual was US$34.21 million, of which US$ US$18.77 million was from IBRD) This component was to finance the upgrading and rehabilitation of rural roads in select counties in two municipalities – Chengdu and Ya’an – in Sichuan Province. This component was to finance the upgrading and rehabilitation of pavements, drainage ditches, bridges, culverts, guardrails, traffic signs, and slope protection in these rural roads. In addition, this component was to finance road safety audits for these investments to inform design. At restructuring, this component was reduced by dropping the Yinglu rural road in Yingjing County. The National Forestry and Grassland Administration planned a new National Panda Park that would overlap with the area of the Yinglu Rural Road. The overlap was expected to bring uncertain environmental risks and would change the safeguards category from B (partial assessment) to A (full assessment). Thus, this contract was dropped so as not to delay the project. Restructuring reallocated the US$5.6 million to emergency shelters in the urban area of Yingjing County in component 2 below.\n2. Upgrading of Priority Urban and Emergency Infrastructure (Total cost at appraisal was US$304.19 million, of which US$245.38 million was from IBRD; restructuring increased this cost to US$370.71 million, of which US$235 million was from IBRD; actual was US$313.49 million, of which US$246.80 was from IBRD) This component was to finance urban infrastructure investments in select counties and districts in the Municipality of Ya’an. Investments were to include reconstruction of urban roads with associated civil works, construction of roads with associated civil works, bridges, emergency evacuation shelters with associated facilities and equipment, upgrading of river embankments, and construction of a water plant. In addition, this component was to finance the conduct of road safety audits for the above-mentioned road infrastructure investments to guide design. Restructuring added US$5.6 million to finance emergency shelters in the urban area of Yingjing County from the cancelled funds under the first component. Restructuring also added government counterpart financing to focus on new investments for stronger flood-resilient infrastructure improvements against record-high flood events in Ya’an Municipality in August 2019.\nIndependent Evaluation Group (IEG) Implementation Completion Report (ICR) Review\nLushan Earthquake Reconstruction Project (P153548)\nPage 3 of 22\n3. Technical Assistance for Strengthening of Disaster Management and Preparedness in Shimian County, later in Ya'an Municipality (Total cost at appraisal was US$4.50 million, all from IBRD; restructuring increased this to US$22.11 million, with US$20.5 million from IBRD; actual was US$18.27 million, all from IBRD). This component was to finance technical assistance to design and implement a pilot disaster risk management (DRM) system for Shimian County and establish a disaster control and command center for the county’s emergency response office. This component was to finance hardware and software to establish the DRM system, and train and build capacity of system users. A Level 1 restructuring (see below) replaced Shimian County (a third level local government administrative hierarchy) with Ya'an Municipality (a second level local government administrative hierarchy).\n4. Project Management and Capacity Building (Total cost at appraisal was US$27.02 million, consisting of US$19.42 million from IBRD; actual cost was US$12.24 million, all from IBRD). This component was to finance surveys and detailed design for project activities. In addition, this component was to finance project management, procurement, engineering supervision,...\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：World Bank Sichuan earthquake reconstruction evaluation\n\n网页标题：Lushan Earthquake Reconstruction and Risk Reduction Project (P153548)\n\n网页链接：http://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099105012222216769/pdf/P1535480dc367708b0a4290c86ac98f9e3d.pdf\n\n网页内容：Upgrading and Risk Reduction of Rural Roads:(Cost $34.83 M) Upgrading of Priority Urban and Emergency Infrastructure:(Cost $375.71 M) Technical Assistance for Strengthening of Disaster Management and Preparedness in Ya'an Municipality:(Cost $22.11 M) Project Management and Capacity Building:(Cost $27.02 M)\nOverall Ratings\nName Previous Rating Current Rating\nProgress towards achievement of PDO Satisfactory Satisfactory\nOverall Implementation Progress (IP) Satisfactory Satisfactory\nOverall Risk Rating Moderate Moderate\nImplementation Status and Key Decisions\nThe Project Development Objective (PDO) is to improve access to disaster resilient infrastructure and to strengthen risk reduction in selected municipalities of Sichuan Province, and to improve emergency preparedness in Ya'an Municipality of Sichuan Province. This objective remains highly relevant to the national, local, and World Bank strategies for China. As of December 2022, 363,406 people in Ya’an Municipality benefit from improved access to urban infrastructure that is regarded as lifelines in the event of an emergency; 131,000 villagers are benefiting from two completed rural roads in Tianquan and Qionglai; 100,000 people in targeted urban areas are provided with improved access to roads; 86,000 people in targeted urban areas are provided with improved drainage conditions; and 61,400 people have improved access to emergency shelters. The project also has improved the urban resilience management of Ya’an Municipality by enhancing the capacity of local authorities in Shimian\nPublic Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized Public Disclosure Authorized\nThe World Bank Implementation Status \u0026 Results Report Lushan Earthquake Reconstruction and Risk Reduction Project (P153548)\n12/22/2022 Page 2 of 10\nCounty to prepare for multi-hazards by setting up a disaster risk management system for the county. The project’s closing date has been extended to December 31, 2023 to allow completion of all