uly.2018 May.2019 August.2020 October.2020 August.2021 December.2022 June. 2023 December. 2023 2. Project Summary and Objectives\nBackground. The National Slum Upgrading Project (the Project) was requested by the Government of Indonesia (GoI) to address urban poverty and improve basic infrastructure and urban services, particularly in slum areas. Rapid urbanization was driving growth, with 137 million persons (over 50 percent of the total population) lived in cities at appraisal, but Indonesia’s economic return from urbanization was lower relative to its peers in Southeast Asia. Every percentage point of increase in urbanization in Indonesia was\nProject Completion Note\nPrepared on 08/02/24\n2\n*OFFICIAL USE ONLY *OFFICIAL USE ONLY\nassociated with a 1.4 percent increase in growth – roughly half of peer countries in the region (2.7 percent). At appraisal, more than 28 million Indonesians lived below the poverty line, of which 36 percent (over 10 million persons) lived in urban areas. Income inequality was also increasing in urban areas, with the Gini coefficient rising from 0.35 in 1995 to 0.42 in 2011. While poverty rates declined steadily from 2006-2018 nationally, declines in urban areas (6.5 percent) were modest compared to rural areas (25 percent). The total number of urban poor was projected to surpass the rural poor by 2030 if the trend continues, with poverty decline in urban areas far lower than rural areas.1 The country’s earlier progress reducing slums had stalled. Roughly 20 percent of the urban population (29 million persons) were slum dwellers, with more than 50 percent classified as poor. Between 1990 and 2005 Indonesia halved the number of slum dwellers, but the pace of reduction slowed. In 2014, the GoI has identified 38,000 hectares of slums across 3,500 urban wards. On June 24, 2016, the AIIB Board of Directors approved the Project in Indonesia, a USD216.5 million sovereign-backed financing (the Loan) to the Republic of Indonesia (the Borrower). The Loan was signed on September 1, 2016, and declared effective on October 11, 2016. This Project was co-financed with the World Bank (the WB) with its approved loan of USD216.5 million. The Project was part of the welldeveloped country-led program called the National Slum Upgrading Program, popularly known in Indonesia as KOTAKU, as an integrated national collaborative platform financed by central and local governments, the private sector, communities, and donors. The AIIB, the WB, the Asian Development Bank, and the Islamic Development Bank worked on a subset of cities, with the AIIB and WB collaborating in the same areas (covering 154 cities in the central and eastern parts of the country). All activities under KOTAKU were to utilize a single program manual and monitoring and evaluation framework, as developed by the Project. Project Objectives and Components. The original Project objective was “to improve access to urban infrastructure and services in the targeted slums in Indonesia”. The revised Project objective after the September 2020 Project change was “to improve access to urban infrastructure and services in the targeted slums in Indonesia and to reconstruct and strengthen public facilities and settlements in selected disaster affected areas”. Specifically, the Project focused on financing improvements to primary and secondary urban infrastructure2 in selected cities, with institutional strengthening for central government and capacity building for local governments and communities. The Project consists of the following five components:\n(i) Component 1 (Institutional and Policy Development) supported institutional strengthening and\ncapacity building of central government ministries and agencies, which are responsible for the management of the Project.\n1 While Indonesia registered a 25 percent decline in rural poverty between 2004 and 2012, there was a far lower decline of 6.5 percent in urban poverty in the same period.\n2 This included improvements in area level small-scale roads, sanitation, water, and drainage systems, and linking tertiary and household systems with primary and secondary infrastructure through connecting infrastructure.\n(ii) Component 2 (Integrated Planning Support and Capacity Building for Local Governments and Communities) provided support for the recruitment and deployment of experts and community facilitators to improve the capacity of governments and communities in selected cities to design and implement slum improvements, including the development of Slum Improvement Action Plans (SIAPs) and Community Settlement Plans (CSPs).\n(iii) Component 3 (Urban Infrastructure and Services in Selected Cities) carried out sub-projects to support primary and secondary infrastructure improvement and site development as well as construction of connecting infrastructure in selected cities as identified in SIAPs and provided Kelurahan Grants to participating Kelurahans (urban villages in Indonesia) to fund sub-projects for tertiary infrastructure upgrading in selected cities based on a community-driven approach as well as for strengthening community-based livelihoods through the construction of livelihoodenabling facilities and services that are important for slum prevention.\n(iv) Component 4 (Implementation Support and Technical Assistance) provided technical support, management, advisory services and training for project management and stakeholder collaboration at national, provincial and city levels, as well as monitoring and evaluation.\n(v) Component 5 (Contingency for Disaster Response, also referred to as the Contingent Emergency Response Component, or CERC) was included to support preparedness and rapid response measures to address disaster, emergency and/or catastrophic events, as needed, through carrying out sub-projects and/or using the Project implementation arrangements. The CERC was activated in November 2018 to support recovery to a series of earthquakes in 2018 in Lombok and Central Sulawesi, which caused 4,402 fatalities, approximately 170,000 displaced people, and USD1.3 billion in economic losses.\n3. Key Dates\nApproval: June 24, 2016 Signing: September 01, 2016 \nEffective: October 11, 2016 Restructured (if any): September 18, 2020; \nDecember 9, 2022 \nOrig. Closing:...\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：AIIB disaster reconstruction project evaluation\n\n网页标题：Study on disaster reconstruction project performance evaluation based on fuzzy analytic network process\n\n网页链接：https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/5533813\n\n网页内容：Study on disaster reconstruction project performance evaluation based on fuzzy analytic network process\nPublisher: IEEE\nChunling Sun; Ran Bi\nAbstract\nAbstract:\nWenchuan earthquake disaster reconstruction planning is a typical government investment project, the performance evaluation of which is conducive to improve the management ability of government of managing disaster reconstruction projects and enriches the finance expenditure decision-making reliance of disaster reconstruction projects. There may be dependence and feedback among factors of disaster reconstruction performance evaluation. In this study, analytic network process (ANP) was adopted to model a multi-criteria disaster reconstruction performance evaluation indices system with dependence and feedback. Due to complexity in evaluating disaster reconstruction performance, it is difficult for decision-makers to provide exact numerical values for the factors. Therefore, a fuzzy ANP method was taken to calculate the weights of each factor of the model, which can solve problems in an uncertain condition effectively.\nPublished in: 2010 International Symposium on Computer, Communication, Control and Automation (3CA)\nDate of Conference: 05-07 May 2010\nDate Added to IEEE Xplore: 29 July 2010\nISBN Information:\nISSN Information:\nINSPEC Accession Number: 11460683\nDOI: 10.1109/3CA.2010.5533813\nPublisher: IEEE\nConference Location: Tainan, Taiwan\nContents\nAuthors\nReferences\nKeywords\nMetrics\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：AIIB disaster reconstruction project evaluation\n\n网页标题：China: Henan Flood Emergency Rehabilitation and Recovery Project\n\n网页链接：https://www.aiib.org/en/projects/details/2021/approved/China-Henan-Flood-Emergency-Rehabilitation-and-Recovery-Project.html\n\n网页内容：China: Henan Flood Emergency Rehabilitation and Recovery Project\nSUMMARY\nSTATUS\nApproved\nMEMBER\nChina\nSECTOR\nWater\nE\u0026S CATEGORY\nCategory A\nPROJECT NUMBER\n000543\nFINANCING\nAPPROVED FUNDING\nUSD1,000 million\nFINANCING TYPE\nSovereign\nTIMELINE\nCONCEPT REVIEW\nSeptember 8, 2021\nAPPRAISAL REVIEW / FINAL REVIEW\nOctober 13, 2021\nFINANCING APPROVAL\nNovember 25, 2021\nLATEST FIELD VISIT\nJune 2025\nOBJECTIVE\nThe project objectives are to support the post-disaster rehabilitation and recovery in the municipalities of Zhengzhou, Xinxiang, and Jiaozuo of Henan Province, and to strengthen the capacity of the three municipalities in integrated flood risk management and flood emergency response.\nDESCRIPTION\nThis project is an emergency operation in response to the urgent need of the government for post-flood disaster recovery and reconstruction. The project will finance activities in three municipalities of Zhengzhou, Xinxiang and Jiaozuo, which are among the most severely impacted by the flooding. Interventions will focus on rehabilitation and reconstruction of damaged infrastructure while the long-term sustainability and climate resilience are taken into account. The project will support post-disaster recovery in three sectors, which are critical to social and economic recovery of the province. The three sectors are water resources and flood management, urban, and transport.\nComponent 1: Zhengzhou Post-disaster Recovery Program. This component will finance civil works and goods required for rehabilitation and reconstruction activities in urban and rural areas, and some expansion or improvement of the flood and drainage management infrastructure in the city of Zhengzhou. The subcomponents are: (a) integrated Jinshui River management project, (b) rehabilitation of rural roads and bridges, and (c) rehabilitation of the Ying River in Dengfeng City; (d) development of an integrated flood risk management system and strengthening the capacity for emergency response; and (e) project management support for capacity building in procurement, financial management, environmental and social (ES) management and other technical areas.\nComponent 2: Xinxiang Post-disaster Recovery Program. This component will support activities to recover damaged infrastructure in the three sectors in Xinxiang municipality. The subprojects consist of: (a) rehabilitation of rivers and canals including river dredging and dike strengthening; (b) rehabilitation of national and provincial expressways, urban bus terminals and purchase of electrical buses; (c) rehabilitation and improvement of urban drainage systems; (d) development of integrated flood emergency response system; and (e) project management support.\nComponent 3: Jiaozuo Post-disaster Recovery Program. This component will finance interventions to support the recovery of Jiaozuo municipality, including: (a) rehabilitation of rivers and canals including river dredging and dike strengthening; (b) rehabilitation and improvement of the urban canals, drainage and sewage systems, and roads; (c) development of an integrated flood emergency response system including a smart river management system and a smart urban water management system; and (d) project management support.\nENVIRONMENTAL AND SOCIAL INFORMATION\nThe AIIB’s Environmental and Social Policy (ESP), including the Environment and Social Standards (ESSs) and the Environmental and Social Exclusion List is applicable to the proposed project. The Bank’s Environment and Social Standard 1 \u0026 2 (ESS1 \u0026 2) are triggered based on initial screening and due diligence with respect to specific contexts of the subprojects that were proposed by the clients.\nThe proposed project involves rehabilitation and reconstruction of the damaged infrastructure in the three sectors as mentioned above. The project has been assigned Category A, given the number of sub-projects involving construction activities spreading across the three project municipalities. These sub-projects will require environmental and social safeguard measures corresponding to various civil works to be executed during implementation.\nGiven the emergency nature of the project, especially under the COVID-19 pandemic situation, a phased approach, permitted under the ESP for Situations of Urgent Need of Assistance is appropriate, has been considered to address environmental and social risks and impacts. The proposed ES instruments are as follows.\nEnvironmental and Social Management Planning Framework (ESMPF). Since this is an emergency operation and the details of the project scope and activities have not been fully identified, a framework appraoch is approriate. The framework document has been prepared and it covers mainly: (1) background and objectives; (2) description of the project; (3) policy framework on environmental and social issues; (4) approach to addressing ES issues; (5) screening, impact assessment and management for ES issues; (6) institutional arrangement with respective responsibilities and processing procedures; (7) Grievance Redress Mechinism (GRM) and accountability mechanism; and (8) supervision, monitoring and evaluation and reporting arrangements.\nEnvironmental and social impact assessment (ESIA) and environmental and social management plan (ESMP). During project implementation, ESIAs and ESMPs required for those identified sub-projects will be prepared according to screening of ES impacts and risks for the proposed specific project activities, under the guidance of ESMPF. If land acquisition and resettlement are required or associated with any proposed project activities, a resettlement plan (RP) shall be prepared.\nPublic consultations will be held to disseminate information on all environmental and social documentation prepared for the project. A Project level Grievance Redress Mechanism (GRM) will be established in the project areas. All ES documents prepared for the project will be disclosed on the websites of the Government/ Client and AIIB, respectively.\nPROJECT TEAM LEADER\nGuoping Zhang\nSenior Investment Specialist – Water\nguoping.zhang@aiib.org\nBORROWER\nYue Ding\nDeputy Director\nProject Implementation Monitoring Report (October 2024)\nProject Implementation Monitoring Report (April 2024)\nProject Implementation Monitoring Report (October 2023)\nProject Implementation Monitoring Report (March 2023)\nCLIENT/COFINANCING PARTNER DOCUMENTS\nESIA ESMP-Xinxiang Urban (Chinese)\nESIA ESMP-Xinxiang Urban (English)\nResettlement Action Plan-Xinxiang Urban (Chinese)\nResettlement Action Plan-Xinxiang Urban (English)\nEnvironmental and Social Impact Assessment (ESIA) \u0026 Environmental and Social Management Plan (ESMP) (English)\nEnvironmental and Social Impact Assessment (ESIA) \u0026 Environmental and Social Management Plan (ESMP) (Chinese)\nResettlement Action Plan - Dengfeng Ying River (English)\nResettlement Action Plan - Dengfeng Ying River (Chinese)\nEnvironmental and Social Impact Assessment and Management Plan - Dengfeng Ying River (English)\nEnvironmental and Social Impact Assessment and Management Plan - Dengfeng Ying River (Chinese)\nResettlement Action Plan - Jinshui River (English)\nResettlement Action Plan - Jinshui River (Chinese)\nEnvironmental and Social Impact Assessment and Management Plan - Jinshui River (English...\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：AIIB disaster reconstruction project evaluation\n\n网页标题：亚投行贷款河南新乡等地特大暴雨洪涝灾害灾后恢复重建项目——新乡市城区排涝能力提升项目环境和社会影响评价及管理计划\n\n网页链接：http://aiib-live-cn-test.aiib.org/en/projects/details/2024/_download/China/ESIA-ESMP-Xinxiang-Urban-Chinese.pdf\n\n发布时间：2024-09-01T00:00:00+08:00\n\n网页内容：亚投行贷款支持项目亚投行贷款河南新乡等地特大暴雨洪涝灾害灾后恢复重建项目--新乡市城区排涝能力提升项目环